 ## Overview
-Tree64 is a sparse voxel tree structure with a 4³ branching factor, designed to reduce tree depth and memory overhead compared to standard octrees [S1, unofficial][S2]. It achieves higher traversal throughput by skipping 64 voxels per node level, resulting in a tree approximately half as deep as an octree for the same resolution [S2]. It is primarily used for real-time ray tracing of large voxel scenes [S1, unofficial].
+Tree64 is a sparse voxel tree with a $4^3$ branching factor, also known as a contree [S1, unofficial][S2]. Its main advantages over standard octrees are shallower trees and lower overhead per voxel [S1, unofficial]. Traversal is fast because each level skips $4^3$ cells and the tree is half as deep as an octree [S2]. It is primarily used for real-time ray tracing of large voxel scenes [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-Tree64 represents a 3D volume as a tree where each internal node covers a $4 \times 4 \times 4$ sub-volume. Unlike standard octrees that branch by 8, Tree64 nodes branch by 64, allowing for shallower hierarchies and fewer traversal steps [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Tree64 represents a 3D volume as a tree where each internal node covers a $4 \times 4 \times 4$ sub-volume [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard octrees that branch by 8, Tree64 nodes branch by 64, allowing for shallower hierarchies and fewer traversal steps [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
